# Kingfisher GNSS discipline example.
# Merge into /etc/chrony/chrony.conf (Debian / Raspberry Pi OS).
#
# Boot policy: allow the clock to be *stepped* (not just slewed) for the first few
# Step whenever the error exceeds 0.2 s, at ANY time (-1) — not just the first
# few updates. For a flight recorder a rare discrete step is *better* than a
# fast slew: kingfisher's clock watcher logs every step to the flight DB's
# clock_offsets table (exact, correctable discontinuity), whereas a slew at
# chrony's default max rate (8.3%!) smears sample intervals for seconds and
# cannot be corrected post-hoc. maxslewrate 1000 (0.1%) keeps the residual
# sub-0.2 s corrections gentle: a 20 ms sample period distorts ≤ 20 µs.
# Backward steps are acceptable — they are logged; unlogged distortion is not.
makestep 0.2 -1
maxslewrate 1000
rtcsync

# Offline-first flight recording: leave pool commented out.
#pool pool.ntp.org iburst

# gpsd SOCK refclocks. Recent gpsd creates chrony.clk.<device>.sock for serial
# time-of-day and chrony.<pps>.sock for PPS.
#
# IMPORTANT — the GPS serial source is `noselect`:
# The gpsd serial (NMEA) time-of-day has a latency relative to the true second
# that VARIES from boot to boot (seen ~0 on some boots, ~0.6 s on others, as the
# receiver's reported fix epoch wanders). With GPS + PPS both selectable, whenever
# that latency lands far from PPS the two disagree, chrony marks BOTH as
# falsetickers ("#x", "Can't synchronise: no majority"), and the deadlock never
# self-heals — the classic "sometimes locks in seconds, sometimes never" symptom.
# Chasing it with the `offset` directive CANNOT work: the latency is not constant,
# so no fixed offset keeps GPS and PPS agreeing.
#
# Fix: mark the serial source `noselect` so it can never be selected or step the
# clock — it only supplies the time-of-day that PPS `lock GPS` needs to number the
# second. PPS (sub-µs, always correct) becomes the sole timekeeper. Keep
# `offset 0.0` so the serial time stays honest for that second-numbering and for
# the boot-time wrong-second cross-check (see kingfisher-clock-check.sh).
#
# Expected steady state in `chronyc sources`:
#   #? GPS  ...  Reach 377   <- present & reachable, but noselect: NOT an error
#   #* PPS  ...  offset in ns/µs
refclock SOCK /run/chrony.clk.ttyAMA0.sock refid GPS precision 1e-1 offset 0.0 delay 0.1 poll 2 filter 3 noselect

# PPS follow-on (GPIO 18 -> /dev/pps0, also in gpsd DEVICES). Use SOCK PPS —
# do not also enable refclock PPS /dev/pps0; gpsd holds the device.
# Keep PPS poll == GPS poll so lock GPS always has a recent time-of-day sample.
refclock SOCK /run/chrony.pps0.sock refid PPS precision 1e-7 lock GPS poll 2

# --- Alternative profiles (pick one set of refclock lines) ---
#
# UART-only (no PPS in chrony): simplest; ~ms accuracy. Here the serial source IS
# the timekeeper, so it is selectable (no noselect). Use offset 0.0 with RTC battery.
# refclock SOCK /run/chrony.clk.ttyAMA0.sock refid GPS precision 1e-1 offset 0.0 delay 0.1 poll 2 filter 3
#
# PPS-only (no GPS refclock at all): sub-µs. gpsd still needs a GPS fix so the PPS
# SOCK carries absolute UTC seconds. RTC + makestep provide coarse boot time.
# Equivalent to the noselect setup above, but without the serial wrong-second cross-check.
# refclock SOCK /run/chrony.pps0.sock refid PPS precision 1e-7 poll 2
